# engine/hooks.patch — hooks into the pinned ScummVM Drascula engine.
#
# Small, readable edits to the engine's own sources, applied by
# scripts/build-engine.sh with `git apply` against vendor/scummvm. The work
# itself lives in engine/a11y.cpp (copied in by the build script); these hunks
# are only the few lines that construct the A11yLayer and call into it.
#
# Lines starting with `#` above the first `diff` line are comments and are
# ignored by git apply. The build script treats a file with no diff hunks as
# "nothing to apply".
#
# Task 3 hooks (drascula.cpp):
#   * include drascula/a11y.h
#   * construct A11yLayer on the stack at the top of runCurrentChapter(), so it
#     is in scope for both chapter setup and the per-frame main loop
#   * hard-code the intro skip: animation_1_1() is guarded out (R1.4)
#   * call _a11y.onStartup() once the first playable room is entered, to assert
#     subtitles and emit the first A11Y room-state line (R1.4, R6.1)
#
# Task 4 hook (drascula.cpp):
#   * call _a11y.poll() once per frame at the top of the main loop. For now
#     poll() watches for a room/chapter change and re-emits the A11Y room-state
#     line, so the line prints on every room change (R1.1). The transition
#     itself happens in exitRoom()/enterRoom() (rooms.cpp, not patched); control
#     returns to the main loop once the move settles, so detecting the change in
#     poll() keeps all engine hooks in drascula.cpp (R10.2). The command reader
#     and A11Y_DIFF build on this same per-frame entry point in later tasks.
#
# Task 13 hook (drascula.h):
#   * declare `friend class A11yLayer;` on DrasculaEngine. The a11y layer reads
#     the engine's own data to observe state and reproduce its actions. Most of
#     what it needs is public (the room/inventory/flag arrays), but the `inv`
#     listing's "look at it" line (R6.4) comes from the engine's text and
#     room-action tables (`_text`, `_roomActions`, `_roomActionsSize`), which
#     are private. One `friend` line lets the layer READ those exactly as the
#     engine's own roomParse(200) does, rather than duplicating game data or
#     fabricating look lines. It grants no new behaviour — the layer still only
#     calls the engine's own methods — and keeps the patch a single readable
#     line instead of widening the engine's public surface.
#
# Task 14 hooks (text capture, R6.2 / gotcha 11):
#   * drascula.h: forward-declare `class A11yLayer;` (so the pointer member can
#     be declared before a11y.h is included) and add a `A11yLayer *_a11y` member
#     on DrasculaEngine. The layer lives on the stack in runCurrentChapter(),
#     but graphics.cpp's text path (centerText) is a DrasculaEngine method with
#     only `this` in scope, so the layer registers itself through this pointer
#     in its constructor and clears it in its destructor. nullptr-initialised in
#     the engine constructor so a text draw with no layer live is a safe no-op.
#   * drascula.cpp: `_a11y = nullptr;` in the constructor (init before any layer
#     exists).
#   * graphics.cpp: include drascula/a11y.h, and at the top of centerText() —
#     the single chokepoint every on-screen text line passes through (spoken
#     dialogue in talk.cpp, narration in animation.cpp, system text in
#     interface.cpp, hover/examine text in moveCursor) — hand the whole message
#     to _a11y->onText() so the layer emits one `A11Y_TEXT <line>` per distinct
#     line. centerText() is called every frame while a subtitle is on screen, so
#     onText() de-duplicates against the last line it emitted; the string is the
#     engine's own text (English subtitle data, or raw Spanish object names,
#     gotcha 10), emitted verbatim. This captures the character replies that
#     talk.cpp never routes through ScummVM's own TTS (gotcha 11).
#
# Task 15 hooks (conversations, R7.1 / R7.2 / R6.3 / gotcha 4):
#   * converse.cpp: include drascula/a11y.h, and add three small hooks around
#     the engine's own conversation loop. A Drascula conversation is a modal
#     loop inside DrasculaEngine::converse() that pumps its own frames, so the
#     main loop — and A11yLayer::poll() — never runs while it is open (gotcha 4).
#     The hooks are the only way to describe the conversation and apply
#     `choose`/`choices` from inside that loop:
#       - onConverseBegin() just before the `while (breakOut == 0)` loop marks a
#         conversation open and arms the first A11Y_CHOICES.
#       - onConverseFrame(...) just after the four option lines are laid out
#         (phraseN_bottom known) and before the engine tests the cursor against
#         them. The layer caches the band bottoms, (re-)emits A11Y_CHOICES on the
#         opening frame and after each choice (R6.3), and services a queued
#         `choose <n>` by setting _mouseY into option n's band and pressing the
#         left button — the SAME state a real click produces — so the engine's
#         own selection below runs talk()+response() (options 1-3) or breakOut
#         (option 4) for that option this frame (R7.1, R7.2).
#       - onConverseEnd() just after the loop clears the conversation state so
#         `choices`/`choose` typed outside a conversation are an A11Y_ERR again.
#     All the logic lives in engine/a11y.cpp; these are just the call sites, each
#     guarded on _a11y so the engine builds and runs unchanged with no layer.
#
# Task 16 hook (cutscene/speech skipping, drascula.cpp — R7.3 / R7.4 / gotcha 5):
#   * one line at the END of DrasculaEngine::updateEvents(): `if (_a11y)
#     _a11y->onUpdateEvents();`. updateEvents() is the engine's per-frame event
#     pump and the ONE function that runs every frame inside BOTH the speech
#     loops (talk.cpp's `do { ... } while (!isTalkFinished())`, which reach it
#     via getScan()/getAction()) and the cutscene loops (animation.cpp, e.g.
#     animation_2_1 the chapter 1->2 crossing and animation_3_1 the tavern
#     first-visit talk, which call it directly and test `getAction() ==
#     kActionSkip`). A cutscene or speech line runs the engine's OWN loop and
#     does not return to the main loop until it finishes or is skipped, so
#     A11yLayer::poll() — and the command reader it drives — never runs for the
#     whole duration (gotcha 5). Hooking updateEvents() is therefore the only
#     way to deliver a `skip` from the command file while a cutscene blocks
#     poll(). onUpdateEvents() reads the command file (throttled to ~250 ms so a
#     ~10 ms delay loop does not churn the FS) and, when a `skip` is queued,
#     raises the engine's OWN skip input — kActionSkip pushed through
#     addActionToBuffer(), exactly as the keymapper does for the player's skip
#     key — so the engine's existing skip path fires with no new engine logic:
#     a speech line ends (isTalkFinished() stopSound()), a cutscene aborts (its
#     `getAction() == kActionSkip` break). Placed after the event-pump `while`
#     so the injected action is not clobbered the same frame; guarded on _a11y.
